{'review': '{\n  "review": {\n    "overall_status": "NEEDS_FIXES",\n    "quality_score": 5.5,\n    "summary": {\n      "critical": 0,\n      "high": 0,\n      "medium": 6,\n      "low": 2,\n      "passed_checks": [\n        "No hardcoded secrets",\n        "React hooks used correctly",\n        "WebSocket event listeners properly cleaned up"\n      ]\n    },\n    "findings": [\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-001",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "security",\n        "title": "Access token exposed in URL query parameter",\n        "file": "src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts",\n        "line_range": [43, 43],\n        "description": "The access token is appended to the WebSocket URL as a query parameter (e.g., `ws://example.com?access_token=token`). This exposes the token in server logs, browser history, and referrer headers, creating a significant leakage risk.",\n        "impact": "Attackers could steal the token from logs, history, or referrer headers and hijack WebSocket sessions or impersonate the user.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Use short-lived tokens and ensure server does not log query parameters. For better security, consider using WebSocket subprotocols or cookies with SameSite attributes (though browser limitations prevent setting custom headers in WebSocket handshake).",\n        "code_before": "const wsUrlWithToken = `${url}?access_token=${accessToken}`;",\n        "code_after": "const wsUrlWithToken = `${url}?access_token=${accessToken}`; // Mitigation: use short-lived tokens and server-side log sanitization",\n        "references": [\n          "OWASP A02:2021 - Cryptographic Failures",\n          "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTML5_Security_CheatSheet.html#websockets"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-002",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "WebSocket URL construction vulnerable to injection and malformed URLs",\n        "file": "src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts",\n        "line_range": [43, 43],\n        "description": "The access token is inserted directly into the URL without encoding, and the URL is appended with `?access_token=` without checking for existing query parameters. This can break the URL if the token contains special characters (e.g., &, =) or if the URL already has query parameters.",\n        "impact": "Malformed URLs causing WebSocket connection failures. If the token is attacker-controlled (though unlikely), it could allow injection of additional query parameters.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Encode the access token and use the correct URL separator (& if URL already has query string, otherwise ?).",\n        "code_before": "const wsUrlWithToken = `${url}?access_token=${accessToken}`;",\n        "code_after": "const separator = url.includes(\'?\') ? \'&\' : \'?\'; const wsUrlWithToken = `${url}${separator}access_token=${encodeURIComponent(accessToken)}`;",\n        "references": [\n          "OWASP A03:2021 - Injection",\n          "URL Standard: https://url.spec.whatwg.org/"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-003",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Missing error handling for WebSocket constructor",\n        "file": "src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts",\n        "line_range": [45, 45],\n        "description": "The WebSocket constructor is called without a try/catch block. If the URL is invalid (e.g., malformed, unsupported protocol), the constructor will throw an exception, breaking the hook and potentially crashing the component.",\n        "impact": "Uncaught exceptions leading to broken WebSocket connections and poor user experience. The component may fail to render if the error propagates.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Wrap the WebSocket constructor in a try/catch block and handle errors appropriately (e.g., log error, trigger onError callback).",\n        "code_before": "ws.current = new WebSocket(wsUrlWithToken);",\n        "code_after": "try {\\n  ws.current = new WebSocket(wsUrlWithToken);\\n} catch (err) {\\n  console.error(\'Failed to create WebSocket:\', err);\\n  onError?.(new Event(\'error\'));\\n  return;\\n}"\n        },\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-004",\n        "severity": "low",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Missing validation for empty or invalid WebSocket URL",\n        "file": "src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts",\n        "line_range": [28, 28],\n        "description": "The `url` parameter is not validated before being used to construct the WebSocket URL. An empty string or invalid URL will cause the WebSocket constructor to throw.",\n        "impact": "Unnecessary exceptions and connection failures when the URL is missing or malformed.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Add a check for empty or invalid URL at the beginning of the connect function and return early with a warning.",\n        "code_before": "const connect = useCallback(() => {\\n    if (!accessToken) {\\n        console.warn(\\"No access token provided for WebSocket connection.\\");\\n        return;\\n    }\\n    // ... rest of function",\n        "code_after": "const connect = useCallback(() => {\\n    if (!accessToken) {\\n        console.warn(\\"No access token provided for WebSocket connection.\\");\\n        return;\\n    }\\n    if (!url || typeof url !== \'string\' || url.trim() === \'\') {\\n        console.warn(\\"WebSocket URL is not provided or is invalid.\\");\\n        return;\\n    }\\n    // ... rest of function",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-005",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "User-provided callbacks lack error handling",\n        "file": "src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts",\n        "line_range": [47, 67],\n        "description": "The onOpen, onMessage, onError, and onClose callbacks are invoked without try/catch blocks. If any of these callbacks throw an exception, it will propagate and potentially break the WebSocket event handling or React rendering.",\n        "impact": "Uncaught exceptions in user callbacks can disrupt WebSocket reconnection logic, cause memory leaks, or crash the component.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Wrap each callback invocation in a try/catch block to prevent exceptions from propagating.",\n        "code_before": "ws.current.onopen = (event) => {\\n      console.log(\\"WebSocket connected.\\");\\n      setIsConnected(true);\\n      reconnectAttempts.current = 0;\\n      onOpen?.(event);\\n    };",\n        "code_after": "ws.current.onopen = (event) => {\\n      try {\\n        console.log(\\"WebSocket connected.\\");\\n        setIsConnected(true);\\n        reconnectAttempts.current = 0;\\n        onOpen?.(event);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        console.error(\'Error in onOpen callback:\', err);\\n      }\\n    };",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-006",\n        "severity": "low",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Stale WebSocket reference returned in hook",\n        "file": "src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts",\n        "line_range": [105, 105],\n        "description": "The hook returns `ws: ws.current` which may reference a closed WebSocket object after disconnection. Callers might attempt to use this reference (e.g., call sendMessage) on a closed socket, leading to silent failures or errors.",\n        "impact": "Callers may incorrectly believe they have an active WebSocket connection when it is actually closed, causing messages to fail to send without clear indication.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Set `ws.current = null` when the WebSocket is closed (in onclose handler and disconnect function) to return a null reference when no active connection exists.",\n        "code_before": "ws.current.onclose = (event) => {\\n      console.log(\\"WebSocket disconnected:\\", event.code, event.reason);\\n      setIsConnected(false);\\n      onClose?.(event);\\n\\n      if (!event.wasClean && reconnectAttempts.current < reconnectLimit) {\\n        reconnectAttempts.current++;\\n        console.log(`Attempting to reconnect in ${reconnectInterval / 1000}s... (Attempt ${reconnectAttempts.current}/${reconnectLimit})`);\\n        timeoutId.current = setTimeout(connect, reconnectInterval);\\n      }\\n    };",\n        "code_after": "ws.current.onclose = (event) => {\\n      ws.current = null; // Mark as closed\\n      console.log(\\"WebSocket disconnected:\\", event.code, event.reason);\\n      setIsConnected(false);\\n      onClose?.(event);\\n\\n      if (!event.wasClean && reconnectAttempts.current < reconnectLimit) {\\n        reconnectAttempts.current++;\\n        console.log(`Attempting to reconnect in ${reconnectInterval / 1000}s... (Attempt ${reconnectAttempts.current}/${reconnectLimit})`);\\n        timeoutId.current = setTimeout(connect, reconnectInterval);\\n      }\\n    };",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-007",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Missing isMounted check for state setters",\n        "file": "src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts",\n        "line_range": [22, 22],\n        "description": "State setters (setIsConnected, setMessage) are called in WebSocket event handlers that may fire after the component has unmounted. This can cause memory leaks and React warnings about state updates on unmounted components.",\n        "impact": "Memory leaks and degraded performance in applications with frequent component mounting/unmounting (e.g., route changes).",\n        "suggested_fix": "Add an isMounted ref to track component mount status and check it before setting state.",\n        "code_before": "export const useWebSocket = (url: string, accessToken: string | null, options?: WebSocketOptions) => {\\n  const {\\n    onOpen,\\n    onMessage,\\n    onError,\\n    onClose,\\n    reconnectInterval = 3000,\\n    reconnectLimit = 5,\\n  } = options || {};\\n\\n  const ws = useRef<WebSocket | null>(null);\\n  const [isConnected, setIsConnected] = useState(false);\\n  const [message, setMessage] = useState<string | null>(null);\\n  const reconnectAttempts = useRef(0);\\n  const timeoutId = useRef<NodeJS.Timeout | null>(null);\\n  // ... rest of hook",\n        "code_after": "export const useWebSocket = (url: string, accessToken: string | null, options?: WebSocketOptions) => {\\n  const {\\n    onOpen,\\n    onMessage,\\n    onError,\\n    onClose,\\n    reconnectInterval = 3000,\\n    reconnectLimit = 5,\\n  } = options || {};\\n\\n  const ws = useRef<WebSocket | null>(null);\\n  const [isConnected, setIsConnected] = useState(false);\\n  const [message, setMessage] = useState<string | null>(null);\\n  const reconnectAttempts = useRef(0);\\n  const timeoutId = useRef<NodeJS.Timeout | null>(null);\\n  const isMounted = useRef(true);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    return () => {\\n      isMounted.current = false;\\n    };\\n  }, []);\\n  // ... rest of hook with isMounted checks before setState",\n        "references": [\n          "React documentation: https://reactjs.org/docs/hooks-faq.html#how-to-read-an-often-changing-value-from-usestate"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-008",\n        "severity": "low",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "timeoutId ref not reset after clearing timeout",\n        "file": "src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts",\n        "line_range": [80, 82],\n        "description": "In the disconnect function, after clearing a timeout with clearTimeout(timeoutId.current), the timeoutId.current ref is not set to null. This leaves a stale reference to a cleared timeout ID.",\n        "impact": "Low risk, but could cause confusion if the ref is inspected elsewhere. Cleaner practice is to set to null after clearing.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Set timeoutId.current = null after clearing the timeout.",\n        "code_before": "if (timeoutId.current) {\\n        clearTimeout(timeoutId.current);\\n      }",\n        "code_after": "if (timeoutId.current) {\\n        clearTimeout(timeoutId.current);\\n        timeoutId.current = null;\\n      }",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-009",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Reconnect attempt counter not reset on intentional disconnect",\n        "file": "src/hooks/useWebSocket.ts",\n        "line_range": [77, 84],\n        "description": "The reconnectAttempts counter is only reset to 0 in the onopen handler (on successful connection). When the connection is intentionally disconnected (via disconnect function or useEffect cleanup), the counter is not reset. This means if the token is removed and then re-added, the reconnect attempt count may still be high, preventing a new connection from being established.",\n        "impact": "WebSocket may fail to reconnect after token renewal if the previous session had exhausted the reconnect limit, even though the new session should start fresh.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Reset reconnectAttempts.current to 0 in the disconnect function to ensure intentional disconnections start a fresh connection attempt count.",\n        "code_before": "const disconnect = useCallback(() => {\\n    if (ws.current) {\\n        ws.current.close();\\n        if (timeoutId.current) {\\n          clearTimeout(timeoutId.current);\\n        }\\n      }\\n  }, []);",\n        "code_after": "const disconnect = useCallback(() => {\\n    if (ws.current) {\\n        ws.current.close();\\n        ws.current = null; // Also clear WebSocket reference\\n        reconnectAttempts.current = 0; // Reset reconnect counter\\n    }\\n    if (timeoutId.current) {\\n      clearTimeout(timeoutId.current);\\n      timeoutId.current = null;\\n    }\\n  }, []);",\n        "references": []\n      }\n    ],\n    "positive_observations": [\n      "Proper use of useCallback for memoizing connect/disconnect functions",\n      "Correct cleanup of WebSocket connections and timeouts in useEffect",\n      "Reasonable default values for reconnectInterval and reconnectLimit",\n      "Clear logging for connection lifecycle events",\n      "TypeScript interfaces provide good documentation for options"\n    ]\n  }\n}'}